      "side": 1,
      "message": "Is this supposed to be a question?\n\nThe application note referenced (section 2.7) makes it clear that the following can happen.\n\nThe task is running, it loads r0 and r1 with the two parameters ready for the svc instruction.\n\nThe cpu executes the svc instruction.\n\nThe cpu starts stacking the r0, r1, r2, r3, r12, lr, pc on the current stack.\n\nThe cpu gets an interrupt whilst it is stacking the registers and before it has executed the first instruction of the svc handler.\n\nThen the registers r0, r1, r2, and r3 can be corrupted. The copies on the stack are ok.\n\nThe svc handler is expecting 2 parameters, passed in r0 and r1. Therefore we carefully reload these two values from the stack before we jump to the  svc handler. \n\nThe values in r2 and r3 are \"don\u0027t care\".\n\nWhen the svc handler returns the values of the saved registers should be restored from the values saved on the stack. As the application note makes clear, if you want to return a value from the svc then you need to alter the saved value of r0 (or r0 and r1, or however your calling conventions dictate) rather than just letting the C compiler return them in r0 (or whatever) directly.\n\nPut it another way, the svc_handler should be declared not to return a value.\n\nThe assembler code in __schedule looks fine. Personally I would have written the two instructions in init.S rather than the 3 lines of asm instructions in the C file.",
